    "claim": "Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference to truth or probability, but as a non-inductive methodological rule grounded in the aim of science: maximizing verisimilitude (truth-likeness) through severe testing. The 'cost' of accepting this defense is the abandonment of any guarantee that the preferred theory is true or even probable, requiring scientists to act on a critical rational faith that the unrefuted theory is closer to the truth than its refuted rivals, without empirical proof that past success predicts future reliability.",
